<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Rendering.IrradianceFields</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The irradiance field of docs/plan/19: a sparse grid of bricks, each four probes cubed in
            a five-cubed footprint so hardware trilinear crosses a brick boundary without a seam, a
            pool that holds them, and an indirection grid mapping a world cell to a pool slot. The
            storage and sampling half of dynamic global illumination — written once and deliberately
            ignorant of whether a ray tracer or an offline capture filled it. Device-free, so the
            sampling is checked against arithmetic rather than against a picture.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>global-illumination;irradiance;probes;spherical-harmonics;lumen;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core\Vixen.Core.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Imaging\Vixen.Core.Imaging.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Mathematics\Vixen.Core.Mathematics.csproj" />

        <!--
            For the traced filler, which marches a scene's distance field to find out what a probe
            sees. The arrow doc 19 § 3 draws from the tracing substrate into the irradiance field, and
            it runs one way: the storage half compiles without knowing a tracer exists.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Rendering.DistanceFields\Vixen.Rendering.DistanceFields.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Rendering.IrradianceFields.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
